records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-17-54z-treasury-mts-individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026-fy2026/draft_codex_events.jsonl:50:{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_25", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -lc \"find records/thesis-analyst -type f \\\\( -name 'raw_response.txt' -o -name 'normalized_cells.json' -o -name 'manifest.json' \\\\) | xargs rg -n \\\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026|treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026|fy2025|FY2025\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -120\"", "aggregated_output": "records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US SNAP Participation, Mar 2026\",\"question\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP national Average Participation for March 2026, not seasonally adjusted, first-print fixed vintage\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":41.92,\"ciLow\":40.95,\"ciHigh\":42.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-10\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP Data Tables\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first USDA FNS SNAP monthly national Average Participation value published for March 2026 in the FY2026 national monthly SNAP data table. Convert persons to millions and round to three decimals. Use the first official print only; ignore later revisions or refreshed vintages.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.snap.average_participation.2026-03.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"FY2025 Mar latest visible official, millions\",\"value\":41.759},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Jun latest visible official, millions\",\"value\":41.812},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Sep latest visible official, millions\",\"value\":41.734},{\"label\":\"FY2025 Nov latest available official, millions\",\"value\":41.687}],\"drivers\":[\"SNAP caseloads have been nearly flat around 41.7 million in recent official monthly prints\",\"Judgmental macro and household-budget adjustment adds a small upward tilt into early 2026\",\"Judgmental work-requirement, churn, and recertification adjustment offsets part of that upside\",\"March has limited seasonal signal relative to administrative and policy effects\",\"First-print uncertainty is dominated by state reporting lags, policy implementation, and later revision risk\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/data-research/data-release-calendar\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T13:28:36Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for USDA FNS SNAP March 2026 First-Print Participation\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first USDA Food and Nutrition Service publication of national SNAP Average Participation for March 2026. The target is an administrative count, not seasonally adjusted, resolved from the official FNS SNAP data table rather than FRED or news mirrors.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the USDA FNS SNAP data table page for the national monthly SNAP participation series and latest visible reference class.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official monthly national Average Participation reference points, converted from persons to millions: FY2025 Mar 41.759 million, FY2025 Jun 41.812 million, FY2025 Sep 41.734 million, and FY2025 Nov 41.687 million; the page also showed Latest Available Month November 2025.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official FNS data release calendar / posting schedule for the March 2026 fixed-vintage availability date.\",\"result\":\"Fetched the USDA FNS data release calendar entry for SNAP Program Data / monthly SNAP data table refresh showing the March 2026 first-print fixed vintage scheduled for 2026-07-10.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked USDA FNS SNAP program context for scale and administrative mechanism.\",\"result\":\"Fetched program context showing SNAP serves about 42 million people nationally and is administered through 50 states plus the District of Columbia, which explains state-reporting lag risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: recent official monthly participation is tightly clustered around 41.7 million. A neutral outside-view forecast therefore starts near 41.75 million before adding early-2026 macro and policy adjustments.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Model choice: I did not use a formal time-series model because the latest official points show very low short-run trend signal and the largest uncertainty is administrative/policy implementation; a persistence baseline with explicit judgmental adjustments is adequate for this fast public-release forecast.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level and momentum: the cited latest visible official points range from 41.687 million to 41.812 million, only a 0.125 million span across the selected recent months, so I keep the level close to the 41.75 million baseline and do not extrapolate a large trend.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Current-release adjustments: the +0.20 million macro/need pressure and -0.03 million policy/churn offset are judgmental adjustments, not directly estimated from the FNS historical table. They reflect a small upward demand-risk tilt partly offset by recertification, eligibility, and work-rule churn.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 41.75 million recent baseline + 0.20 million judgmental macro/need pressure - 0.03 million judgmental policy/churn adjustment = 41.92 million. Interval calculation: recent cited official points span only 0.125 million, but the target is several months beyond the latest available print and has policy/state-reporting risk, so I use a judgmental 80% interval about -0.97 to +0.93 million around the point, giving 40.95 to 42.85 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: a downside outside-the-interval case would be rapid enforcement of eligibility or work-rule changes or unusually high procedural churn pushing participation below 40.95 million; an upside outside-the-interval case would be a sharper labor-market deterioration, food-budget stress, or delayed disenrollment pushing participation above 42.85 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: accepted the reviewer requests to identify the release-calendar entry more specifically, state the persistence-model choice, label the macro and policy adjustments as judgmental, tie the interval to recent observed dispersion plus named policy/reporting uncertainty, keep the policy adjustment consistent at -0.03 million, and clarify that displayed historical values are latest visible official values converted to millions.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":41.92,\"ciLow\":40.95,\"ciHigh\":42.85}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-29-23z-usda-fns-wic-total-participants-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:48:   \"Late-FY2025 and early-FY2026 softness argues against fully matching March 2025\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US June 2026 Monthly Treasury Deficit\",\"question\":\"What monthly deficit will the U.S. Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service report in the first-print Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026, Table 1 summary of receipts, outlays, and deficit/surplus, not seasonally adjusted?\",\"unit\":\"usd_billions\",\"pointEstimate\":72,\"ciLow\":-35,\"ciHigh\":210,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-13\",\"resolutionSource\":\"Official agency release\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, Table 1 deficit/surplus for the month of June 2026, with deficit positive and surplus negative, converted from the agency's dollars-in-millions presentation to USD billions and rounded to the nearest 1 billion. Ignore later revisions or annual-report restatements.\",\"dataPointId\":\"treasury.mts.table_1.deficit_surplus.2026-06.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"2025-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":27},{\"label\":\"2024-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":66},{\"label\":\"2023-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":228},{\"label\":\"2026-05 tariff refunds noted\",\"value\":22},{\"label\":\"2026 fiscal-year tariff revenue through May\",\"value\":189}],\"drivers\":[\"June quarterly tax payments usually lift receipts\",\"June 1 payment timing can move outlays across months\",\"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\",\"interest and entitlement outlays remain high\",\"FY2026 deficit was reported slightly below FY2025 through May after calendar adjustment\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/release-calendar/\",\"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\",\"https://www.kiplinger.com/taxes/tariff-stimulus-checks\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-17T02:35:38Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for the June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolver is the first official Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, using the monthly deficit/surplus in Table 1 and treating deficits as positive USD billions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data release calendar page for the release-calendar source used to verify the official schedule surface.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official Fiscal Data release-calendar page showing Release Calendar, copyright 2026, and the statement that it shows estimated dates and times for upcoming data releases; June 2026 MTS is scheduled for 2026-07-13.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data Monthly Treasury Statement dataset page as the official resolution source.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official Monthly Treasury Statement dataset URL for the Table 1 resolver; historical Table 1 June monthly deficits used as reference points were 2025-06: 27 USD billion, 2024-06: 66 USD billion, and 2023-06: 228 USD billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"news.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked contemporaneous reporting on the May 2026 Treasury statement for current fiscal context.\",\"result\":\"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"history.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked reporting around June 2025 tariff and fiscal context as a prior-year anchor.\",\"result\":\"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate step: the most relevant reference class is recent June MTS prints because June contains quarterly estimated tax payments and has strong seasonality. The recent June deficits of 27, 66, and 228 billion imply a wide but positive-deficit center; the two most recent years point closer to 50 billion than to the 2023 outlier.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":72,\"ciLow\":-35,\"ciHigh\":210}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/normalized_cells.json:28:    \"label\": \"FY2025 official/catalog reference\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/normalized_cells.json:66:    \"result\": \"Fetched canonical slug snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027; catalog values include FY2024 973, FY2025 975, FY2026e 994, prior point 1010, prior CI 975 to 1045, and ledger resolutionDate 2026-09-30.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/normalized_cells.json:77:    \"call\": \"Open USDA Guidance Portal entries for FY2024 and FY2025 SNAP COLA issue dates\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/normalized_cells.json:78:    \"result\": \"Fetched FY2024 issued date August 3, 2023 and FY2025 issued date August 2, 2024; FNS pages show FY2024 page updated March 16, 2026 and FY2025 page updated March 6, 2026.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/normalized_cells.json:100:    \"text\": \"Base-rate/reference class: recent first-print household-of-4 values moved 939 to 973 to 975, and the local FY2026 anchor is 994. The year-to-year changes are about +3.6 percent, +0.2 percent, and +1.9 percent, with the current inflation evidence closer to the upper part of that recent range than to the FY2025 near-flat COLA.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-13-16z-usda-fns-snap-maximum-allotment-household-size-4-48dc-fy2027-fy2027/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"SNAP family-4 max allotment FY2027\",\"question\":\"What will the first USDA FNS FY2027 maximum monthly SNAP allotment for a household of 4 in the 48 contiguous states and DC be, in dollars per month?\",\"unit\":\"usd\",\"pointEstimate\":1021,\"ciLow\":1000,\"ciHigh\":1055,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-09-30\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service first official FY2027 SNAP Cost-of-Living Adjustments memorandum or table\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/cost-living-adjustment-cola-information\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first official USDA FNS FY2027 SNAP Cost-of-Living Adjustments memorandum or table value for maximum monthly allotments, household size 4, 48 contiguous states and DC. The 2026-09-30 date is the catalog resolution deadline/latest-by date tied to FY2027 benefits becoming effective October 1, not a claim that publication occurs on that day. If USDA FNS posts the FY2027 table before 2026-09-30, that first official post controls. Use the dollar amount as first published, with no later revision or conversion.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.snap.maximum_allotment.household_size_4.48dc.fy2027.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"FY2023 official first print\",\"value\":939},{\"label\":\"FY2024 official/catalog reference\",\"value\":973},{\"label\":\"FY2025 official/catalog reference\",\"value\":975},{\"label\":\"FY2026 catalog anchor\",\"value\":994}],\"drivers\":[\"Unobserved June 2026 Thrifty Food Plan cost is the mechanical basis\",\"Food-at-home CPI running 2.7 percent year over year in May 2026\",\"ERS 2026 food-at-home forecast midpoint 2.8 percent\",\"Annual FNS COLA table effective October 1\",\"Low but nonzero policy-change risk around TFP rules\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/fy-2023-cola\",\"https://www.usda.gov/guidance-documents/snap/fns/snap-fy-2024-cost-living-adjustments-0\",\"https://www.usda.gov/guidance-documents/snap/fns/snap-fy-2025-cost-living-adjustments\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/cost-living-adjustment-cola-information\",\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm\",\"https://www.ers.usda.gov/data-products/food-price-outlook/summary-findings\",\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-7/subtitle-B/chapter-II/subchapter-C/part-273/subpart-D/section-273.10\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T23:13:24Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"SNAP FY2027 maximum allotment, household of 4\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolver is the first official USDA FNS FY2027 SNAP COLA memorandum or table for the household-size-4 maximum monthly allotment in the 48 contiguous states and DC. The repo already registers the canonical slug snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027 and target date 2026-09-30, so I reuse that rather than creating a near-duplicate.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"local.repo\",\"call\":\"rg and sed over forecast-cells.ts and ledger-targets.generated.ts for the target registration\",\"result\":\"Fetched canonical slug snap-max-allotment-family-4-fy2027; catalog values include FY2024 973, FY2025 975, FY2026e 994, prior point 1010, prior CI 975 to 1045, and ledger resolutionDate 2026-09-30.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.fns.lookup\",\"call\":\"Open USDA FNS SNAP FY2023 COLA page for an official inline reference point and effective-date mechanics\",\"result\":\"Fetched FY2023 memorandum date August 9, 2022, effective Oct. 1, 2022, and household-of-4 48 states/DC maximum allotment 939 dollars.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.usda.guidance.lookup\",\"call\":\"Open USDA Guidance Portal entries for FY2024 and FY2025 SNAP COLA issue dates\",\"result\":\"Fetched FY2024 issued date August 3, 2023 and FY2025 issued date August 2, 2024; FNS pages show FY2024 page updated March 16, 2026 and FY2025 page updated March 6, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.ecfr.lookup\",\"call\":\"Open 7 CFR 273.10 maximum SNAP allotment rule\",\"result\":\"Fetched 7 CFR 273.10 current as of 6/24/2026; maximum allotments are adjusted annually, posted in an FNS web table, effective October 1, and based on 100% of the preceding June TFP rounded down to the nearest lower dollar.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.bls.lookup\",\"call\":\"Open BLS May 2026 CPI release for current food-at-home momentum\",\"result\":\"Fetched BLS May 2026 CPI: all items +0.5 percent monthly and +4.2 percent year over year; food at home +0.1 percent monthly and +2.7 percent year over year; June 2026 CPI scheduled for July 14, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.ers.lookup\",\"call\":\"Open USDA ERS June 2026 Food Price Outlook summary\",\"result\":\"Fetched ERS June 2026 forecast: food-at-home prices predicted +2.8 percent in 2026 with 95 percent prediction interval 1.4 to 4.4 percent; all food +3.2 percent with interval 2.2 to 4.2 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference class: recent first-print household-of-4 values moved 939 to 973 to 975, and the local FY2026 anchor is 994. The year-to-year changes are about +3.6 percent, +0.2 percent, and +1.9 percent, with the current inflation evidence closer to the upper part of that recent range than to the FY2025 near-flat COLA.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: the best level anchor is the FY2026 catalog anchor of 994 dollars. Current grocery inflation has not collapsed; BLS shows 2.7 percent year-over-year food-at-home inflation in May 2026 and ERS puts 2026 food-at-home inflation at 2.8 percent. The eCFR rule makes this a mechanical TFP/June-cost update unless Congress or USDA changes the TFP basis before the FY2027 table.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point: 994 dollars FY2026 anchor x 1.027 May food-at-home momentum = 1020.8, rounded to 1021 dollars. Interval: translate uncertainty around June 2026 TFP movement, publication rounding down to whole dollars, and modest policy/mechanical risk into about +0.6% to +6.1% from the 994 anchor, giving 994 x 1.006 = 1000 and 994 x 1.061 = 1055. This is wider than the ERS 1.4% to 4.4% food-at-home prediction interval because the resolver is the TFP basket, not CPI itself, and because policy or basket-specific food movements can add tail risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Catalog-prior reconciliation: the local prior point of 1010 and CI of 975 to 1045 looked too low after incorporating the 994 FY2026 anchor plus May 2026 food-at-home CPI at 2.7 percent and ERS 2026 food-at-home midpoint at 2.8 percent, so I shift the point up to 1021 and move the interval to 1000 to 1055 while keeping a similar uncertainty width.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: below 1000 would require roughly less than +0.6 percent growth from the FY2026 anchor, plausibly from a sharp June 2026 TFP softening or a policy freeze/rebase. Above 1055 would require roughly more than +6.1 percent growth, plausibly from a fresh TFP reevaluation, commodity shock, or food-at-home spike several points above current CPI and ERS signals.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver clarification that 2026-09-30 is a catalog deadline/latest-by date, tightened the interval explanation around June 2026 TFP movement, rounding, and policy risk, and added reconciliation from the catalog prior to the updated 1021 point estimate.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":1021,\"ciLow\":1000,\"ciHigh\":1055}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-58z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:32:    \"label\": \"FY2025 deficit\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-58z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:91:    \"result\": \"May 2024 deficit was $347 billion; FY2024 deficit was $1.833 trillion; FY2025 deficit was $1.775 trillion.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-39-40z-usda-fns-snap-persons-april-2026-april-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"Recent FY2026 participation is materially below FY2025 levels\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-39-40z-usda-fns-snap-persons-april-2026-april-2026-fixed-vintage/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"usda-fns-snap-persons-april-2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"SNAP persons April 2026\",\"question\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons, national total, first posted fixed-vintage print converted to millions of persons.\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":39.8,\"ciLow\":38.4,\"ciHigh\":41.2,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-24\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service SNAP Data Tables\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first USDA FNS SNAP monthly data-table print for the FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons field, national total. Convert the published person count to millions by dividing by 1,000,000. Use the first posted fixed-vintage USDA FNS value; ignore later revisions unless the first print is formally withdrawn before scoring.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.snap.persons.2026-04.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"Apr 2023 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":41.91381},{\"label\":\"Apr 2024 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":41.612145},{\"label\":\"Nov 2024 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":43.018848},{\"label\":\"Apr 2025 persons, current table at lookup, millions\",\"value\":42.353149},{\"label\":\"Oct 2025 persons, latest FY2026 table, millions\",\"value\":41.0918},{\"label\":\"Nov 2025 persons, latest available month on fetched page, millions\",\"value\":40.395735}],\"drivers\":[\"Recent FY2026 participation is materially below FY2025 levels\",\"April same-month reference class has been near 42 million but latest months imply a lower level\",\"SNAP participation is countercyclical, limiting downside if labor-market conditions soften\",\"State reporting and disaster-assistance timing can move first-print monthly totals\",\"Policy and administrative changes may continue to reduce caseloads into spring 2026\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/sites/default/files/resource-files/snap-4fymonthly-2.pdf\",\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/data-research/data-release-calendar\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T13:41:51Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast USDA FNS SNAP persons for April 2026 first print\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The target is the national SNAP FY2026 National View Summary monthly table, April 2026 row, Participation Persons field, resolved from USDA FNS SNAP Data Tables on the first public monthly print and converted from persons to millions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened USDA FNS SNAP Data Tables page and checked the national monthly table status.\",\"result\":\"The page listed FY 23 through FY 26 National View Summary with Latest Available Month: November 2025 and Page updated: February 24, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the USDA FNS SNAP monthly PDF linked from the SNAP Data Tables page.\",\"result\":\"Fetched current-table April same-month persons counts: Apr 2023 41,913,810; Apr 2024 41,612,145; Apr 2025 42,353,149.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Read the prior-year and latest FY2026 rows in the USDA FNS SNAP monthly PDF.\",\"result\":\"Fetched Nov 2024 persons count 43,018,848 and FY2026 persons counts: Oct 2025 41,091,800; Nov 2025 40,395,735; the table also showed FY2026 average 40,743,768 for the available months.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official FNS data release calendar for the resolver date.\",\"result\":\"The official release schedule placed the SNAP monthly data update covering April 2026 on 2026-07-24, which is 1 scheduled release date for this first-print target.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate reference class: April observations in the fetched current table for 2023-2025 were 41.914 million, 41.612 million, and 42.353 million, so the simple same-month base rate is about 41.96 million before current-release adjustments.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Current-level adjustment: Nov 2025 was 40.395735 million versus Nov 2024 43.018848 million, a ratio of 0.9390. Applying that ratio to Apr 2025 42.353149 million gives 39.77 million; rounding and allowing partial stabilization gives a 39.8 million point estimate.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: the sharp Oct-Nov 2025 drop may include timing, reporting, or temporary administrative noise; SNAP is also countercyclical, so a softer economy could keep April 2026 closer to 41 million than the recent downtrend implies.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Downside outside scenario: continued eligibility tightening, administrative churn, or unusually low state reporting pushes the first print below 38.4 million. Upside outside scenario: reporting rebound, disaster assistance, or weaker household income conditions lift participation above 41.2 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Interval sizing: the fetched April same-month 2023-2025 range is 0.741 million and the Oct-Nov 2025 move is -0.696 million, but policy and first-print reporting risk make a wider judgmental 80% half-width appropriate. I use +/-1.4 million around 39.8, about twice those recent realized moves, for an 80% interval of 38.4 to 41.2 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver clarification by naming the exact FY2026 National View Summary monthly table and Participation Persons field, accepted adding Nov 2024 evidence before the ratio calculation, accepted making the interval width explicitly judgmental and tied to fetched realized moves, and clarified that historical values are current-table lookup values while resolution uses the first posted April 2026 print.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":39.8,\"ciLow\":38.4,\"ciHigh\":41.2}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:20:    \"label\": \"FY2025 Mar latest visible official, millions\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:24:    \"label\": \"FY2025 Jun latest visible official, millions\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:28:    \"label\": \"FY2025 Sep latest visible official, millions\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:32:    \"label\": \"FY2025 Nov latest available official, millions\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-25-26z-usda-fns-snap-participation-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/normalized_cells.json:62:    \"result\": \"Fetched official monthly national Average Participation reference points, converted from persons to millions: FY2025 Mar 41.759 million, FY2025 Jun 41.812 million, FY2025 Sep 41.734 million, and FY2025 Nov 41.687 million; the page also showed Latest Available Month November 2025.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-29-23z-usda-fns-wic-total-participants-march-2026-fixed-vintage-march-2026-fixed-vintage/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"usda-fns-wic-total-participants-march-2026-fixed-vintage\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US WIC participants, Mar 2026\",\"question\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service WIC total participants for March 2026, national monthly data, first preliminary print/fixed vintage, not revised values\",\"unit\":\"count\",\"pointEstimate\":6686000,\"ciLow\":6580000,\"ciHigh\":6795000,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-06-12\",\"resolutionSource\":\"USDA Food and Nutrition Service WIC Participation and Costs monthly data, FY 2026 preliminary fixed vintage\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/sites/default/files/resource-files/37wic-monthly-6.pdf\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first USDA FNS WIC Participation and Costs national monthly-data print that includes March 2026 total participants, using the published whole-participant count in the Total Participants column. Do not use later revised vintages, FRED, or mirrors; revisions after the first March 2026 print are ignored.\",\"dataPointId\":\"usda.fns.wic.total_participants.2026-03.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"Feb 2026 prior official vintage\",\"value\":6640819},{\"label\":\"Mar 2025 official comparison\",\"value\":6850836},{\"label\":\"Feb 2025 official comparison\",\"value\":6802175},{\"label\":\"Apr 2025 official comparison\",\"value\":6877215},{\"label\":\"Mar 2024 official comparison\",\"value\":6680974},{\"label\":\"Mar 2023 official comparison\",\"value\":6621311}],\"drivers\":[\"February 2026 prior-vintage level was below the FY 2025 comparable month\",\"March usually rebounds from February in WIC participation\",\"Recent March observations clustered from roughly 6.62 million to 6.85 million participants\",\"Late-FY2025 and early-FY2026 softness argues against fully matching March 2025\",\"Administrative reporting and state caseload timing can move the first print by tens of thousands\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.fns.usda.gov/pd/wic-program\",\"https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/sites/default/files/resource-files/37wic-monthly-5.pdf\",\"https://fns-prod.azureedge.us/sites/default/files/resource-files/37wic-monthly-6.pdf\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T13:31:35Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"USDA FNS WIC total participants, March 2026 fixed vintage\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the USDA Food and Nutrition Service WIC Participation and Costs national monthly table, Total Participants, March 2026, first preliminary print. The forecast evidence excludes the March 2026 realized value and uses only the official source family, the prior official vintage, and earlier monthly observations.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the USDA FNS WIC Data Tables page to identify the official source family and monthly national data resources.\",\"result\":\"The official WIC Data Tables page provides monthly national WIC data resources for FY 2026 preliminary reporting and earlier official comparison data; the WIC program served 6,640,819 participants in Feb 2026 in the prior fetched vintage.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Fetched the immediately prior official vintage, 37wic-monthly-5.pdf, to establish the latest pre-target information available before the March 2026 first print.\",\"result\":\"The prior official vintage states Data as of May 8, 2026 and includes Feb 2026 Total Participants 6,640,819 while not including Mar 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Fetched official year-earlier and adjacent-month reference points for seasonal and level context.\",\"result\":\"Official comparison totals were Feb 2025 6,802,175, Mar 2025 6,850,836, and Apr 2025 6,877,215 participants.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Fetched older March official observations to size the recent reference class.\",\"result\":\"Older official March totals included Mar 2024 6,680,974 and Mar 2023 6,621,311 participants.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official resolution vintage metadata for the first print containing March 2026, using it only for resolver timing and not as forecast evidence.\",\"result\":\"The March-inclusive official FNS resolution vintage is dated June 12, 2026 and is the first fetched official vintage after the May 8, 2026 prior vintage; the target row is resolved from that June 12 print.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class view: a seasonal-naive prior starts from Feb 2026 and adds a typical February-to-March increase. In 2025 the increase was 48,661, while the recent March level range was 6,621,311 to 6,850,836 across 2023-2025.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Model prior: use a simple seasonal persistence model, averaging two anchors: Feb 2026 plus a roughly 45,000 March seasonal rebound, and Mar 2025 adjusted down by the Feb 2026 year-over-year shortfall versus Feb 2025. This intentionally avoids a more complex time-series model because only a small monthly official reference class is needed for this first-print target.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Current-release update: Feb 2026 at 6,640,819 was 161,356 below Feb 2025, so carrying the full year-over-year shortfall to March gives about 6,689,480. A direct Feb-to-Mar seasonal rebound anchor gives about 6,685,000. The point estimate is placed near the midpoint of those pre-release anchors.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Upside scenario: March administrative reporting catches up more than usual, participation retains more of the 2025 level, or state caseload timing adds a larger-than-normal seasonal bounce, putting the first print near or above 6.75 million. Downside scenario: the early-2026 decline persists, the March seasonal bounce is weak, or reporting delays hold the print near 6.60 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: 0.5 * (6,640,819 + 45,000) + 0.5 * (6,850,836 - 161,356) = 6,687,650, rounded to 6,686,000 after a small downward adjustment for late-2025 and early-2026 softness. The 80% interval is 6,580,000 to 6,795,000, spanning roughly plus 154,000 and minus 106,000 around the point to cover recent first-print seasonal dispersion, reporting timing, and a mildly upside-skewed rebound risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: Accepted the blocking leakage, update, interval, coherence, and model-prior critiques by removing the realized March 2026 value from evidence and historicalContext, widening the interval to ex-ante forecast uncertainty, and adding a seasonal-persistence prior. Kept the official resolver metadata and June 12 resolution source only for resolution clarity.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":6686000,\"ciLow\":6580000,\"ciHigh\":6795000}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"FY2026 deficit was reported slightly below FY2025 through May after calendar adjustment\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-58z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"us-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US June 2026 Monthly Deficit\",\"question\":\"What will the U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement first print report for the June 2026 total federal budget deficit or surplus, measured as outlays minus receipts, with deficits positive and surpluses negative?\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":45000,\"ciLow\":-80000,\"ciHigh\":190000,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-13\",\"resolutionSource\":\"U.S. Department of the Treasury, Bureau of the Fiscal Service, Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/reports-statements/mts/\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, using total federal outlays minus total federal receipts from the Summary of Receipts, Outlays, and the Deficit/Surplus. Record in USD millions at the agency's published precision. If Treasury labels the month as a surplus, record a negative deficit. Ignore later revisions or annual Combined Statement updates.\",\"dataPointId\":\"treasury.mts.monthly_deficit.2026-06.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"2025-06\",\"value\":-27000},{\"label\":\"2024-06\",\"value\":66000},{\"label\":\"2024-05\",\"value\":347000},{\"label\":\"FY2025 deficit\",\"value\":1775000},{\"label\":\"FY2024 deficit\",\"value\":1833000}],\"drivers\":[\"June estimated-tax receipts usually improve the monthly balance\",\"Tariff/customs receipts were elevated in June 2025 and may remain a partial offset\",\"Net interest and mandatory spending keep the deficit baseline high\",\"Calendar timing of benefit payments can move tens of billions between months\",\"Tax legislation and enforcement changes add uncertainty to receipts\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/reports-statements/mts/\",\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/monthly-budget-review\",\"https://www.investopedia.com/how-trumps-tariff-revenue-helped-us-government-make-bank-in-june-11770789\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-17T02:20:10Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"US Treasury June 2026 Monthly Deficit Forecast\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first official Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026. I express the target as outlays minus receipts, so a Treasury surplus is a negative deficit.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement page for the official release family and first-print source.\",\"result\":\"Official MTS source identified; the June 2026 print is resolved from Treasury's MTS tables, with values published in millions of dollars.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the official FiscalData Monthly Treasury Statement dataset page for the agency data surface and units.\",\"result\":\"FiscalData Monthly Treasury Statement tables provide receipts, outlays, and deficit/surplus fields in dollars or millions-equivalent table values; resolver unit set to USD millions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.calendar\",\"call\":\"Checked the official MTS release schedule convention for the June 2026 statement release date.\",\"result\":\"The June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement is scheduled for 2026-07-13, the first official print date used for resolution.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"history.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked recent June budget-balance history and tariff context from public reporting tied to Treasury's June 2025 MTS release.\",\"result\":\"June 2025 showed a $27 billion surplus, tariff receipts were $26.6 billion in June 2025 versus $6.3 billion in June 2024, and June 2025 interest payments were about $84 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"history.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked recent monthly and fiscal-year deficit context from public budget reporting and Treasury/CBO summaries.\",\"result\":\"May 2024 deficit was $347 billion; FY2024 deficit was $1.833 trillion; FY2025 deficit was $1.775 trillion.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base rate: June is a high-receipt month because estimated individual and corporate tax payments arrive, so June deficits are usually smaller than adjacent months and can become surpluses. The last two June anchors point to a range from a $66 billion deficit in 2024 to a $27 billion surplus in 2025.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: the 2025 June surplus had unusually favorable ingredients, including a large spending drop and sharply higher customs receipts. By June 2026, higher interest costs and normalized agency spending could pull the month back into deficit even if tariff revenue remains elevated.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"I start from the midpoint of the June 2024 deficit (+66,000 million) and June 2025 surplus (-27,000 million), about +19,500 million. I add roughly +25,000 million for higher net interest and spending normalization, yielding a rounded point of +45,000 million. An 80% interval of -80,000 to +190,000 million allows for tax-payment timing, benefit-payment shifts, and tariff-receipt uncertainty.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":45000,\"ciLow\":-80000,\"ciHigh\":190000}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-17-54z-treasury-mts-individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026-fy2026/draft_codex_events.jsonl:53:{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_27", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"tariff revenue|customs duties|individual income tax|refund|receipts were|outlays were|FYTD receipts|fiscal-year\\\" records/thesis-analyst site/src/data -g '*.json' -g '*.ts' -g '*.txt' | head -200\"", "aggregated_output": "site/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:593:          '{ point: 13.0, ci80: [12.1, 14.1], drivers: [\"ctc_refundability\", \"earnings\", \"housing_costs\"] }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:643:        text: \"The official poverty measure excludes taxes, refundable credits, and noncash benefits, so it is a cleaner near-term read on cash-income and labor-market strength than SPM.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1049:        text: \"The SPM child poverty rate for 2027 is dominated by the configuration of refundable credits under whatever tax regime is in force, plus the labor market. The CTC is the single largest policy lever \u2014 it directly raised the CTC sensitivity of SPM child poverty by roughly 4 percentage points during the 2021 expansion. The 2027 reading depends critically on how TCJA-extension legislation resolves.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1062:          '{ point: 11.4, ci80: [10.5, 12.4], drivers: [\"CTC remains $2,000 / child\", \"$1,700 refundable portion preserved\", \"EITC parameters unchanged\"] }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1068:          '{ point: 12.2, ci80: [11.3, 13.1], drivers: [\"CTC at $1,500 / child\", \"refundability cap retained\", \"EITC reverts\"] }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1084:        text: \"House passed a TCJA extension package in March. Senate markup is expected Q3. Conditional on the Senate moving a vehicle, full extension is the modal outcome but partial scaling (especially on CTC refundability) is a live possibility. Weighting:\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1550:    title: \"Federal individual income tax revenue, FY2027\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1552:      \"What will total federal individual income tax revenue be for fiscal year 2027 in nominal dollars, as published in the Monthly Treasury Statement?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1562:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2027 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2027, in billions of nominal dollars.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1840:        text: \"Maximum is the headline parameter \u2014 refundability and phase-in are separate forecast cells. Current law: $2,000/child through TY2025, reverting to $1,000/child in TY2026 absent legislation. Any TCJA extension would lock at $2,000 (= $167/mo) or higher.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1883:    title: \"Cost of a $3,000 fully refundable CTC, TY2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1885:      \"What will official scorers estimate as the federal budget cost of a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026, relative to current law?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1895:      \"Resolves to the first official JCT or CBO estimate, in billions of nominal dollars, for the tax-year 2026 federal budget effect of a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit scored against current law.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1896:    policyParameter: \"gov.irs.credits.ctc.refundable.fully_refundable\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1957:      \"Resolves to federal outlays/refundable-credit cost attributable to the Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS/Treasury table that separately identifies the CTC.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1975:        text: \"This is the baseline CTC outlay cell the expansion-cost forecast compares against. It needs a tax-year measure, not a fiscal-year cash-flow proxy, because official outlay timing can shift refunds across fiscal years.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1983:          '{ point: 58.8, ci80: [54.0, 64.5], drivers: [\"eligible children\", \"refundability cap\", \"phase-in earnings\"] }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2016:      \"Resolves to federal outlays/refundable-credit cost attributable to the Earned Income Tax Credit for tax year 2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS/Treasury table that separately identifies the EITC.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2046:        call: 'irs.lookup({ table: \"soi_historical_refundable_credits\", credit: \"eitc\", years: [2021, 2024] })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2063:      \"If the SALT deduction cap is set at $40,000 for joint filers in tax year 2027, what will the federal individual income tax revenue loss be relative to a $10,000 cap?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2251:    title: \"Top individual income tax rate, TY2027\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2253:      \"What will the top federal individual income tax marginal rate be for tax year 2027?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2262:      \"Resolves to the highest statutory federal individual income tax marginal rate applicable for TY2027, excluding surtaxes such as NIIT or Medicare taxes.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2464:      \"Resolves to federal SNAP benefit outlays for FY2027, excluding administrative costs, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official USDA FNS or Treasury fiscal-year table that identifies SNAP benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2521:      \"Resolves to federal outlays for refundable premium tax credits under the Affordable Care Act for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CBO table that separately identifies the credit.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2694:      \"Resolves to federal unemployment insurance benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or Department of Labor fiscal-year table that reports UI benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2751:      \"Resolves to federal SSI benefit payments for FY2027, excluding state supplements, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports SSI federal payments.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2807:      \"Resolves to federal Medicaid outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CMS fiscal-year table that reports federal Medicaid payments.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2899:        text: \"The interval keeps a small upside tail for outreach or refundability changes that pull non-filers into the tax system.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3033:      \"Resolves to OASDI benefit payments for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3076:    slug: \"aotc-refundable-outlays-ty2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3080:      \"What will federal refundable American Opportunity Tax Credit outlays be for tax year 2026, in nominal dollars?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3090:      \"Resolves to refundable American Opportunity Tax Credit outlays for TY2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS SOI or Treasury table that separately identifies the refundable AOTC amount.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3092:      \"gov.irs.credits.education.american_opportunity.refundable\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3106:      { kind: \"heading\", text: \"Identifying the refundable-credit target\" },\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3114:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ policy: \"current_law\", year: 2026, output: \"refundable_aotc\", unit: \"billions\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3121:        call: 'irs.lookup({ table: \"soi_refundable_education_credits\", credit: \"aotc\", years: [2019, 2025] })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3315:      \"Resolves to federal Medicare benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury, CMS, or CBO fiscal-year table that reports Medicare benefit payments.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3372:      \"Resolves to federal TANF outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official HHS ACF or Treasury table that reports federal TANF fiscal-year spending.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3530:      \"How many individual income tax returns will claim the Earned Income Tax Credit for tax year 2027?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3539:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2027 individual income tax returns claiming the Earned Income Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2027 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3814:      \"Resolves to the fiscal-year average number of families receiving TANF or Separate State Program Maintenance-of-Effort cash assistance in FY2027, in millions, using the first ACF TANF caseload table covering all FY2027 months.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4251:      \"Resolves to federal School Breakfast Program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS child nutrition program table that reports fiscal-year federal cost.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4361:      \"Resolves to Federal Pell Grant program outlays or obligations for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Department of Education Federal Student Aid annual report, budget justification, or Pell Grant program data table that reports fiscal-year spending.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4416:      \"Resolves to total federal WIC program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS WIC participation and cost table that reports fiscal-year total cost.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4572:      \"How many individual income tax returns will claim the federal child and dependent care credit for tax year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4581:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the federal child and dependent care credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income individual income tax table that reports this credit.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4599:        text: \"The child and dependent care credit is a direct PolicyEngine-relevant tax unit output. Claimant returns are useful because the nonrefundable credit can have stable claims even when revenue cost changes with income and tax liability.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4627:      \"How many individual income tax returns will claim the federal premium tax credit for tax year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4636:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the premium tax credit or reconciling advance premium tax credits on Form 8962, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4682:      \"How many individual income tax returns will claim the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4691:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4700:      \"CTC refundability formula\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4709:        text: \"ACTC claimant returns isolate the refundable part of the CTC. That is the channel that matters most for child poverty and for whether a CTC expansion reaches low-income families.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4722:          '{ point: 16.1, ci80: [13.6, 19.2], drivers: [\"phase_in\", \"refundability_cap\", \"eligible_children\"] }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4737:      \"How much will individual income tax filers deduct for charitable contributions on itemized returns for tax year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4746:      \"Resolves to total charitable contributions deducted on TY2026 itemized individual income tax returns, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income Schedule A or itemized-deductions table covering TY2026.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4897:    slug: \"ctc-refundable-amount-ty2027\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4901:      \"What will the maximum refundable portion of the federal Child Tax Credit be per qualifying child in tax year 2027?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4910:      \"Resolves to the maximum refundable federal Child Tax Credit amount per qualifying child for TY2027, using IRC section 24 and the first IRS Form 1040 instructions or inflation-adjusted tax parameter release that identifies the refundable amount.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4911:    policyParameter: \"irc.24.refundable_amount_per_child.2027\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4928:        text: \"The maximum refundable CTC amount is the policy parameter that drives outlays and child-poverty effects for low-income families. It is distinct from the headline maximum credit because families with low earnings can be limited by the refundable cap and phase-in.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4934:        call: 'axiom.query({ statute: \"IRC.24\", parameter: \"refundable_amount_per_child\", year: 2027, branch: \"post_sunset_current_law\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4936:          '{ value: 1000, note: \"credit reverts and refundable structure narrows under scheduled law\" }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4941:        call: 'axiom.query({ statute: \"IRC.24\", parameter: \"refundable_amount_per_child\", year: 2027, branch: \"tcja_extended_indexed\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4943:          '{ value: 1800, note: \"current refundable amount retained and indexed\" }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4947:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \"ctc_refundable_amount_1800\", year: 2027, output: [\"ctc_outlays\", \"spm_child_poverty_rate\"], unit: \"billions\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4949:          '{ outlays: 64.0, child_poverty_rate: 11.6, sensitivity: \"about $5B per additional $100 refundable cap near the phase-in margin\" }',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4954:        text: \"P(indexed current cap) = 0.55; P($1,000 reversion) = 0.20; P(larger refundable expansion) = 0.20; P(other) = 0.05.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4958:        text: \"The modal outcome is retaining the current refundable design with indexation. The lower tail remains real because the scheduled-law branch is a discrete cliff; the upper tail reflects a child-credit compromise that expands refundability without restoring the full 2021 policy.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5158:      \"Conditional on a full TCJA extension package (CTC at $2,000, current refundability, current EITC) being enacted by June 30, 2026, what will the SPM child poverty rate be in 2028?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5187:        text: \"Conditional resolution: only meaningful if a TCJA-extension package matching the House framework is enacted by June 30, 2026. Under that conditioning event, the CTC ($2,000/child, $1,700 refundable) and EITC are stable through 2028. Question is the residual SPM child-poverty path under stable policy.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5220:    slug: \"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5222:    title: \"Child poverty 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5224:      \"Conditional on a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the Supplemental Poverty Measure child poverty rate be for calendar year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5233:      \"Resolves to the official Census SPM child poverty rate for calendar year 2026, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable CTC being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5235:      \"$3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for tax year 2026\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5254:        text: \"This cell asks what the observed SPM child poverty rate would be if the $3,000 fully refundable CTC policy is actually in force for TY2026. Policy uncertainty is removed; macro and measurement uncertainty remain.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5259:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \"ctc_3000_fully_refundable\", year: 2026, output: \"spm_child_poverty_rate\", map_to: \"person\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5283:      \"Federal individual income tax revenue FY2028 | SALT cap fully repealed\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5285:      \"Conditional on the SALT deduction cap being repealed in full (no cap) for tax years 2026 and later, what will federal individual income tax revenue be in FY2028 (in billions of nominal dollars)?\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5295:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2028 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2028, conditional on the SALT deduction cap under IRC \u00a7164(b)(6) being fully eliminated for TY2026 and later. If the cap is not fully eliminated, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:130:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2027 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2027, in billions of nominal dollars.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:134:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Federal individual income tax revenue, FY2027.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:194:      \"Resolves to federal SNAP benefit outlays for FY2027, excluding administrative costs, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official USDA FNS or Treasury fiscal-year table that identifies SNAP benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:210:      \"Resolves to federal outlays for refundable premium tax credits under the Affordable Care Act for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CBO table that separately identifies the credit.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:258:      \"Resolves to federal unemployment insurance benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or Department of Labor fiscal-year table that reports UI benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:275:      \"Resolves to federal SSI benefit payments for FY2027, excluding state supplements, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports SSI federal payments.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:291:      \"Resolves to federal Medicaid outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CMS fiscal-year table that reports federal Medicaid payments.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:357:      \"Resolves to OASDI benefit payments for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance benefits.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:422:      \"Resolves to federal Medicare benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury, CMS, or CBO fiscal-year table that reports Medicare benefit payments.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:439:      \"Resolves to federal TANF outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official HHS ACF or Treasury table that reports federal TANF fiscal-year spending.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:487:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2027 individual income tax returns claiming the Earned Income Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2027 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:567:      \"Resolves to the fiscal-year average number of families receiving TANF or Separate State Program Maintenance-of-Effort cash assistance in FY2027, in millions, using the first ACF TANF caseload table covering all FY2027 months.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:695:      \"Resolves to federal School Breakfast Program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS child nutrition program table that reports fiscal-year federal cost.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:728:      \"Resolves to Federal Pell Grant program outlays or obligations for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Department of Education Federal Student Aid annual report, budget justification, or Pell Grant program data table that reports fiscal-year spending.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:744:      \"Resolves to total federal WIC program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS WIC participation and cost table that reports fiscal-year total cost.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:792:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the federal child and dependent care credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income individual income tax table that reports this credit.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:808:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the premium tax credit or reconciling advance premium tax credits on Form 8962, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:824:      \"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:840:      \"Resolves to total charitable contributions deducted on TY2026 itemized individual income tax returns, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income Schedule A or itemized-deductions table covering TY2026.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1202:      \"irs.filing_season.total_amount_refunded.october_2026.first_print\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1204:      \"obs.irs.filing_season.total_amount_refunded.october_2026.first_print\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1211:      \"Resolves to IRS Filing Season Statistics total amount refunded for current-year individual returns in the first October 2026 snapshot. If IRS publishes multiple October snapshots, the earliest October table governs.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1215:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for IRS cumulative refunds, October 2026.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2300:    dataPointId: \"treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2301:    observationId: \"obs.treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2309:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax refunds for fiscal year 2026 in the final Monthly Treasury Statement for September 2026. If Treasury revises the table after the initial final MTS release, the first final fiscal-year table governs.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2313:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Individual income tax refunds, FY2026.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2324:      \"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2326:      \"Resolves to the IRS SOI tax-year 2025 estimate for net premium tax credit reconciliation on individual income tax returns, defined as additional net PTC claimed minus excess advance PTC repaid. If IRS publishes separate line-item and ACA-statistics estimates, the ACA-statistics table governs.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2330:      \"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2417:      \"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If such a policy is in effect, this baseline scenario is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2428:      \"cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2430:      \"obs.cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2439:      \"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, this scenario is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2445:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Infant mortality 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6721:      \"Resolves to the official Census SPM child poverty rate for calendar year 2026, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable CTC being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6725:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Child poverty 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6738:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2028 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2028, conditional on the SALT deduction cap under IRC \u00a7164(b)(6) being fully eliminated for TY2026 and later. If the cap is not fully eliminated, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6742:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Federal individual income tax revenue FY2028 | SALT cap fully repealed.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2155:  \"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable\": [\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2158:        \"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable-thesis-analyst-fast-2026-06-27t14-21-25z\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2168:        \"A fully refundable $3,000 CTC directly raises SPM resources through refundable tax credits\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2169:        \"2021 shows large child-poverty sensitivity to refundable credits, but also included stimulus and larger young-child credits\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2588:          text: \"Forecast for 2026 child SPM poverty conditional on a refundable $3,000 CTC\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2592:          text: \"The resolver is the Census Bureau first print for the Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate among people under age 18 for calendar year 2026. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources include taxes, refundable credits, transfers and noncash benefits, and subtract medical, work, and child-care expenses while adjusting thresholds for housing costs.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2599:            \"Fetched 2021 child SPM poverty rate 5.2 percent, 2020 child SPM 9.7 percent, refundable tax credits moving 9.6 million people out of SPM poverty, and stimulus payments moving 8.9 million people out.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2606:            \"Fetched 2022 child SPM poverty rate 12.4 percent, up from 5.2 percent in 2021; overall SPM rate 12.4 percent; refundable tax credits moved 6.4 million people out of SPM poverty, down from 9.6 million in 2021.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2631:          text: \"Base-rate/reference class: without the 2021 expanded credit and stimulus environment, recent child SPM poverty sits around 12.4 to 13.7 percent. I use 13.4 percent as the current-policy outside-view anchor before adding the conditional $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2635:          text: \"Time-series prior: I do not extrapolate a simple linear trend because the child SPM series has large policy discontinuities from pandemic stimulus, expanded refundable credits, and their expiration; a persistence prior around the recent non-expanded-CTC level is more defensible.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2639:          text: \"Policy mechanism: a fully refundable $3,000 CTC would count in SPM tax-credit resources for eligible families and would matter most for children in low-earnings households who receive little or no current-law nonrefundable credit. It should not mechanically recreate the 2021 5.2 percent rate because the 2021 outcome also reflected larger under-age-6 credits, stimulus payments, and pandemic-era safety-net conditions.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2643:          text: \"Anchor 13.4 percent. Subtract 4.7 percentage points for the $3,000 fully refundable CTC, smaller than the 7.2 point 2021-to-2022 child SPM reversal because this condition excludes stimulus and the $3,600 young-child amount. Point = 13.4 - 4.7 = 8.7. For the 80% interval, combine about 1.3 points of recent annual child-SPM movement, roughly 1.5-2.0 points of policy-effect uncertainty, and macro/SPM modeling risk, giving an asymmetric judgmental interval of 5.7 to 12.0.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2647:          text: \"Counter-consideration: an outcome near 5.7 percent or lower could happen if wage growth is broad, housing and food burdens ease, and refundable-credit take-up is near-complete. An outcome near 12.0 percent or higher could happen if unemployment rises, rent or medical out-of-pocket costs lift SPM needs, or filing and implementation frictions limit receipt among low-income families.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2674:        \"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, SNAP, housing subsidies, work expenses, medical expenses, and geographic housing adjustments\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2675:        \"TCJA-style extension avoids a large scheduled child tax credit drop but does not recreate the fully refundable 2021 expansion\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3152:          text: \"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the relevant prior is not a 2022-2024 child series because the draft evidence did not fetch the 2024 child value. I use 2022 and 2023 child SPM values plus the 2024 overall SPM signal. After the 2021 refundable-credit expansion expired, child SPM returned to low-to-mid teens, with 2021 at 5.2% treated as an exceptional policy year.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3164:          text: \"Policy mechanism: under the condition, a House-style TCJA extension prevents the child credit from falling back toward the pre-TCJA $1,000 structure, which is poverty-reducing versus expiration. But the extension is not the 2021 fully refundable CTC and therefore does much less for the lowest-income children than the ARPA design that produced the 5.2% child SPM rate.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3168:          text: \"Counter-consideration: if the enacted package preserves more SNAP, Medicaid-linked expense protection, or refundable child credit value than assumed, child SPM could fall toward 11%. Conversely, deeper nutrition cuts, weak employment, or high shelter inflation by 2028 could push the first print above 18%.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3172:          text: \"Start with a post-expansion base of 13.0% to 13.3% from 12.4% in 2022, 13.7% in 2023, and the 12.9% overall SPM signal for 2024. Add about 0.4 percentage point for partial-refundable-credit limits versus a stronger anti-poverty design and about 0.5 point for likely transfer and expense headwinds under the House framework by 2028, giving 14.2%. The interval 10.8% to 18.0% allows roughly -3.4 to +3.8 points around the point, wider than the observed 2022-to-2023 child SPM move of 1.3 points because the horizon is four years, the target is conditional on fiscal legislation, and labor, housing, and medical-expense shocks can compound.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8506:          text: \"The resolver is the Census Bureau official poverty measure for calendar year 2025, reported as the percentage of people in poverty in the first Poverty in the United States: 2025 release/Table A-1 from CPS ASEC. This is not the SPM: taxes, refundable credits, SNAP, housing-cost adjustments, work expenses, medical expenses, and geographic threshold adjustments do not define this official-poverty target.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9999:            \"Census reported Social Security moved 28.7 million people out of SPM poverty in 2024 and medical expenses moved more than 7.0 million people into SPM poverty in 2024; these figures matter less directly for children than refundable credits, SNAP, housing, child care, and payroll-tax effects.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10023:          text: \"Counter-consideration: upside risk is meaningful if CPS ASEC measured earnings for low-income families disappoint, housing and child care expenses push more families below SPM thresholds, or benefit participation/resource measurement weakens. Downside risk comes from stronger low-wage earnings, lower inflation thresholds, and larger refundable-credit effects than expected.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10031:          text: \"Scenario bounds: a low-side case near 11.7 percent would require strong low-income family earnings and favorable refundable-credit or benefit measurement; a high-side case near 15.4 percent would require weak measured earnings plus housing, child care, medical, or work-expense effects that reduce SPM resources. Values outside the interval would likely need either a recession-scale income miss or an unexpectedly large policy/resource effect.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10058:        \"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10060:        \"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10434:          text: \"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, and noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10466:          text: \"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10474:          text: \"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10478:          text: \"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate above 17 percent, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13148:        \"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13169:          \"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13275:            \"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13282:            \"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13290:          text: \"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13294:          text: \"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13318:        \"tariff-refund shock reduces a revenue tailwind\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13439:            \"June 2025 receipts were 526.445 billion, outlays were 499.435 billion, and the deficit/surplus was -27.010 billion; FYTD receipts were 4008.146 billion, FYTD outlays were 5345.518 billion, and FYTD deficit was 1337.372 billion.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13446:            \"June 2024 receipts were 466.255 billion, outlays were 532.220 billion, and the deficit was 65.965 billion; June 2023 receipts were 418.317 billion, outlays were 646.085 billion, and the deficit was 227.768 billion.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13453:            \"June 2022 receipts were 460.761 billion, outlays were 549.603 billion, and the deficit was 88.842 billion; FYTD deficit through June 2022 was 515.067 billion.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13460:            \"Reports cited Treasury figures that May 2026 net tariff revenue was 0 billion after 22 billion collected and 22 billion refunded, FYTD net tariff revenue was 189 billion versus 81 billion a year earlier, adjusted FYTD deficit was 2 percent or 24 billion below the prior year, and Treasury's April-June 2026 borrowing estimate was 189 billion.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13468:          text: \"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13472:          text: \"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:5:    slug: \"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:7:    title: \"Individual income tax refunds, FY2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:9:      \"What will total federal individual income tax refunds be in fiscal year 2026 as reported by the U.S. Treasury?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:19:      \"Resolves to total individual income tax refunds for fiscal year 2026 in the final Monthly Treasury Statement for September 2026. If Treasury revises the table after the initial final MTS release, the first final fiscal-year table governs.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:20:    dataPointId: \"treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:38:        text: \"Refunds resolve quickly through the Monthly Treasury Statement and give Thesis Institute agents a tax-administration target that connects filing behavior, withholding, and refundable-credit policy.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:43:        call: 'treasury.lookup({ table: \"monthly_treasury_statement\", series: \"individual_income_tax_refunds\", fiscal_years: [2021, 2025] })',\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:49:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ year: 2026, output: \"individual_income_tax_refunds_cash\", timing: \"fiscal_year\", map_to: \"tax_unit\" })',\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:51:          '{ point: 482, ci80: [454, 520], drivers: [\"ctc_refundability\", \"eitc_claiming\", \"withholding_gap\"] }',\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:55:        text: \"The central estimate keeps refunds slightly below the FY2024 spike but above FY2025. The interval widens for filing-season timing because a few weeks of delayed refunds can move dollars across fiscal years without changing tax-year liability.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:74:      \"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:76:      \"Resolves to the IRS SOI tax-year 2025 estimate for net premium tax credit reconciliation on individual income tax returns, defined as additional net PTC claimed minus excess advance PTC repaid. If IRS publishes separate line-item and ACA-statistics estimates, the ACA-statistics table governs.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:122:      \"How many individual income tax returns will claim the Saver's Credit for tax year 2025 in IRS Statistics of Income data?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:168:        text: \"The forecast expects mild growth as real earnings stabilize and contribution rates recover, but it keeps the lower tail near the recent trough because the credit remains nonrefundable and low-salience.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US June 2026 Monthly Treasury Deficit\",\"question\":\"What monthly deficit will the U.S. Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service report in the first-print Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026, Table 1 summary of receipts, outlays, and deficit/surplus, not seasonally adjusted?\",\"unit\":\"usd_billions\",\"pointEstimate\":72,\"ciLow\":-35,\"ciHigh\":210,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-13\",\"resolutionSource\":\"Official agency release\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, Table 1 deficit/surplus for the month of June 2026, with deficit positive and surplus negative, converted from the agency's dollars-in-millions presentation to USD billions and rounded to the nearest 1 billion. Ignore later revisions or annual-report restatements.\",\"dataPointId\":\"treasury.mts.table_1.deficit_surplus.2026-06.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"2025-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":27},{\"label\":\"2024-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":66},{\"label\":\"2023-06 monthly deficit\",\"value\":228},{\"label\":\"2026-05 tariff refunds noted\",\"value\":22},{\"label\":\"2026 fiscal-year tariff revenue through May\",\"value\":189}],\"drivers\":[\"June quarterly tax payments usually lift receipts\",\"June 1 payment timing can move outlays across months\",\"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\",\"interest and entitlement outlays remain high\",\"FY2026 deficit was reported slightly below FY2025 through May after calendar adjustment\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/release-calendar/\",\"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\",\"https://www.kiplinger.com/taxes/tariff-stimulus-checks\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-17T02:35:38Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for the June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolver is the first official Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, using the monthly deficit/surplus in Table 1 and treating deficits as positive USD billions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data release calendar page for the release-calendar source used to verify the official schedule surface.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official Fiscal Data release-calendar page showing Release Calendar, copyright 2026, and the statement that it shows estimated dates and times for upcoming data releases; June 2026 MTS is scheduled for 2026-07-13.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data Monthly Treasury Statement dataset page as the official resolution source.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official Monthly Treasury Statement dataset URL for the Table 1 resolver; historical Table 1 June monthly deficits used as reference points were 2025-06: 27 USD billion, 2024-06: 66 USD billion, and 2023-06: 228 USD billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"news.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked contemporaneous reporting on the May 2026 Treasury statement for current fiscal context.\",\"result\":\"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"history.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked reporting around June 2025 tariff and fiscal context as a prior-year anchor.\",\"result\":\"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate step: the most relevant reference class is recent June MTS prints because June contains quarterly estimated tax payments and has strong seasonality. The recent June deficits of 27, 66, and 228 billion imply a wide but positive-deficit center; the two most recent years point closer to 50 billion than to the 2023 outlier.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":72,\"ciLow\":-35,\"ciHigh\":210}]}\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:180:      \"Conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the U.S. infant mortality rate be for calendar year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:191:      \"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If such a policy is in effect, this baseline scenario is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:193:      \"No materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for TY2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:208:      \"No large refundable CTC income shock\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:214:        text: \"This cell targets the final NCHS/NVSS linked birth/infant death rate: deaths under age 1 per 1,000 live births. The baseline scenario is current-law CTC policy, so a $3,000 fully refundable CTC would make this scenario unresolved rather than wrong.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:238:    slug: \"infant-mortality-rate-2026-ctc-3000-refundable\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:240:    title: \"Infant mortality 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:242:      \"Conditional on a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the U.S. infant mortality rate be for calendar year 2026?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:253:      \"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, this scenario is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:255:      \"$3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for TY2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:257:      \"cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:258:    policyParameter: \"irc.24.ctc_3000_fully_refundable.2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:278:        text: \"This scenario asks about the observed national infant mortality rate if the $3,000 fully refundable CTC is actually in force for TY2026. The official NCHS rate still resolves the value; the condition determines whether this scenario is active.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:282:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \"ctc_3000_fully_refundable\", year: 2026, output: \"cash_transfer_to_households_with_infants\", map_to: \"household\" })',\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:32:    \"label\": \"2026-05 tariff refunds noted\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:36:    \"label\": \"2026 fiscal-year tariff revenue through May\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:43:   \"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:50:   \"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:79:    \"result\": \"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:85:    \"result\": \"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:97:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
