records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-17-54z-treasury-mts-individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026-fy2026/draft_codex_events.jsonl:124:{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "agent_message", "text": "{\"slug\":\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"Individual income tax refunds, FY2026\",\"question\":\"What will the U.S. Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement Table 4 report as current fiscal year-to-date refunds deducted from Total -- Individual Income Taxes for fiscal year 2026 in the first final September 2026 print, in nominal USD billions?\",\"unit\":\"usd_billions\",\"pointEstimate\":360,\"ciLow\":325,\"ciHigh\":410,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-10-20\",\"resolutionSource\":\"U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement, September 2026\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first official final Monthly Treasury Statement for September 2026, Table 4, row Total -- Individual Income Taxes, Current Fiscal Year to Date column Refunds (Deduct). Convert the published $ millions value to USD billions and round to the nearest 0.001 billion for scoring. Ignore later revisions, Combined Statement restatements, or IRS filing-season refund tables.\",\"dataPointId\":\"treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"FY2025 MTS Table 4 individual income tax refunds\",\"value\":327.268},{\"label\":\"FY2024 MTS Table 4 individual income tax refunds\",\"value\":299.426},{\"label\":\"FY2023 MTS Table 4 individual income tax refunds\",\"value\":373.321},{\"label\":\"FY2022 MTS Table 4 individual income tax refunds\",\"value\":246.316},{\"label\":\"FY2021 MTS Table 4 individual income tax refunds\",\"value\":278.436}],\"drivers\":[\"FY2025 final MTS refunds were 327.268 billion, above FY2024 but below the FY2023 spike\",\"mid-2026 filing-season evidence points to materially larger refund dollars than 2025\",\"refund timing can move cash refunds across fiscal years without changing tax-year liability\",\"withholding, senior deduction, tip and overtime provisions can increase overpayment refunds\",\"IRS staffing and direct-deposit transition create processing-delay downside for FY2026 cash timing\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/previous\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0925.pdf\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0924.pdf\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0922.pdf\",\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/release-calendar/\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-27T23:18:02Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"FY2026 MTS individual income tax refunds\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolver is the first final September 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement, not an IRS filing-season table. The target is Table 4 refunds deducted from Total -- Individual Income Taxes, current fiscal year to date, converted from millions to billions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement page and previous-issues page for the official source family and table location.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official MTS source text showing the MTS summarizes receipts, outlays, surplus or deficit, and means of financing; the page was last updated January 15, 2026, and previous issues include FY2025, FY2024, FY2023, FY2022, and FY2021 final September statements.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the FY2025 final September MTS PDF and read Table 4 Total -- Individual Income Taxes.\",\"result\":\"Fetched FY2025 Table 4 Total -- Individual Income Taxes: gross receipts 2983.312 billion, refunds deducted 327.268 billion, and net receipts 2656.044 billion; September 2025 this-month refunds were 8.430 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the FY2024 final September MTS PDF and read Table 4 Total -- Individual Income Taxes plus prior-period FY2023 context.\",\"result\":\"Fetched FY2024 Table 4 Total -- Individual Income Taxes: gross receipts 2725.493 billion, refunds deducted 299.426 billion, and net receipts 2426.067 billion; the same table's prior FY2023 refunds were 373.321 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened FY2022 and FY2021 final September MTS PDFs for older refund reference points.\",\"result\":\"Fetched FY2022 Table 4 individual income tax refunds deducted 246.316 billion from gross receipts 2878.461 billion; fetched FY2021 refunds deducted 278.436 billion from gross receipts 2322.813 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.calendar\",\"call\":\"Checked the Fiscal Data release calendar page for the September 2026 MTS release schedule and the MTS publication timing note in official MTS PDFs.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official Fiscal Data release calendar surface showing Release Calendar, copyright 2026, and the upcoming-data-release schedule; September 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement is scheduled for 2026-10-20, and MTS PDFs state the statement is normally released on the 8th workday after the reporting month.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference class: the clean official MTS Table 4 refund history is 278.436, 246.316, 373.321, 299.426, and 327.268 billion for FY2021-FY2025. The five-year average is about 304.0 billion, while the latest two years average 313.3 billion and the latest year is 327.3 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level and momentum: FY2025 is the best level anchor because refund definitions match the resolver and it already incorporates the post-pandemic filing baseline. FY2025 refunds were 9.3 percent above FY2024, and FY2026 filing-season reports point to larger refund dollars, so a flat repeat would understate the central case.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Policy and timing mechanism: larger tax-year 2025 refundability, deductions, and withholding mismatch can raise FY2026 cash refunds, but the MTS cash concept is sensitive to processing timing. Direct-deposit transition and staffing constraints could push some refunds later or leave them unresolved within FY2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: start with FY2025 MTS Table 4 refunds of 327.268 billion. Apply a judgmental +10 percent current-release uplift, smaller than the reported midseason refund-dollar surge because some early-season gains can be timing, giving 327.268 x 1.10 = 360.0 billion. Interval: use 325 to 410 billion, spanning a near-repeat of FY2025 at the low end and a return toward the FY2023 spike plus policy-driven growth at the high end.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: if the observed 2026 filing-season refund-dollar increase is mostly accelerated processing rather than higher final overpayments, FY2026 could land close to FY2025 or below 325 billion. The upside outside-the-interval case is a broad tax-law-driven refund increase plus smooth processing that pushes Table 4 refunds above 410 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":360,\"ciLow\":325,\"ciHigh\":410}]}"}}
