t\":\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":72,\"ciLow\":-35,\"ciHigh\":210}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"US monthly Treasury deficit, June 2026\",\"question\":\"What will the U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement report as the total on-budget and off-budget deficit or surplus for June 2026, in the first print, with deficits positive and surpluses negative?\",\"unit\":\"usd_billions\",\"pointEstimate\":155,\"ciLow\":20,\"ciHigh\":310,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-07-13\",\"resolutionSource\":\"Official U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, Table 1 total deficit/surplus for FY 2026 June. Convert the published $ millions to $ billions and record deficits as positive and surpluses as negative. Do not revise for later restatements or annual combined statements.\",\"dataPointId\":\"treasury.mts.table1.total_deficit_surplus.2026-06.first_print\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"2025-06 first-print deficit\",\"value\":-27.01},{\"label\":\"2024-06 first-print deficit\",\"value\":65.965},{\"label\":\"2023-06 first-print deficit\",\"value\":227.768},{\"label\":\"2022-06 first-print deficit\",\"value\":88.842},{\"label\":\"2026-Q2 Treasury borrowing estimate\",\"value\":189}],\"drivers\":[\"June corporate tax receipts partially offset outlays\",\"June 1, 2026 falls on a business day, so fewer payments should shift into May than in 2024 or 2025\",\"higher net interest and entitlement outlays than recent years\",\"tariff-refund shock reduces a revenue tailwind\",\"FYTD deficit was reported slightly below the prior year through May after calendar adjustment\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/previous\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0625.pdf\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0624.pdf\",\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0622.pdf\",\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-06-21T15:08:15Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolver: use the first official U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026, Table 1 total deficit/surplus, converted from $ millions to $ billions, with deficits positive and surpluses negative.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement landing page and current-issue links.\",\"result\":\"The official MTS page says the statement covers receipts, outlays, surplus or deficit, and means of financing; it also says data moved to FiscalData on 2025-11-25 and the MTS page was last updated 2026-01-15.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the June 2025 official MTS PDF and read Table 1 plus highlights.\",\"result\":\"June 2025 receipts were 526.445 billion, outlays were 499.435 billion, and the deficit/surplus was -27.010 billion; FYTD receipts were 4008.146 billion, FYTD outlays were 5345.518 billion, and FYTD deficit was 1337.372 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the June 2024 official MTS PDF and read Table 1.\",\"result\":\"June 2024 receipts were 466.255 billion, outlays were 532.220 billion, and the deficit was 65.965 billion; June 2023 receipts were 418.317 billion, outlays were 646.085 billion, and the deficit was 227.768 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Opened the June 2022 official MTS PDF and read Table 1 and the summary chart.\",\"result\":\"June 2022 receipts were 460.761 billion, outlays were 549.603 billion, and the deficit was 88.842 billion; FYTD deficit through June 2022 was 515.067 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"public.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked public reporting on Treasury's May 2026 fiscal context and quarterly borrowing estimates.\",\"result\":\"Reports cited Treasury figures that May 2026 net tariff revenue was 0 billion after 22 billion collected and 22 billion refunded, FYTD net tariff revenue was 189 billion versus 81 billion a year earlier, adjusted FYTD deficit was 2 percent or 24 billion below the prior year, and Treasury's April-June 2026 borrowing estimate was 189 billion.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate: recent June deficits are volatile because corporate tax collections and payment-date shifts matter. The 2022-2025 June outcomes were 88.842, 227.768, 65.965, and -27.010 billion, giving a simple four-year average near 88.9 billion and a three-year average near 88.2 billion, but the 2024 and 2025 June figures were held down by June 1 weekend payment acceleration into May.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":155,\"ciLow\":20,\"ciHigh\":310}]}\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:101:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:105:        \"text\": \"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:101:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:105:    \"text\": \"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:101:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
