e","High construction costs and feasibility constraints cap upside"],"sourceContext":["https://www.abs.gov.au/release-calendar/future-releases/202607","https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/industry/building-and-construction/building-approvals-australia/latest-release","https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/industry/building-and-construction/building-approvals-australia/mar-2026","https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/industry/building-and-construction/building-approvals-australia/feb-2026","https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/industry/building-and-construction/building-approvals-australia/jan-2026"],"runAt":"2026-06-27T13:11:37Z","reasoning":[{"kind":"heading","text":"Forecast May 2026 ABS total dwelling approvals monthly change"},{"kind":"text","text":"The resolver is the first ABS print for Building Approvals, Australia, May 2026: seasonally adjusted total dwelling units approved, monthly percent change. The July 8 updated-information item is excluded because the target is first print."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"ABS July 2026 future release calendar for Building Approvals, Australia May 2026","result":"Fetched official schedule: Building Approvals, Australia, reference period May 2026, Wednesday 01 July 2026 at 11:30am AEST; updated information for the same May 2026 reference period is scheduled Wednesday 08 July 2026 at 11:30am AEST."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"ABS April 2026 Building Approvals latest release key statistics and table","result":"Fetched April 2026 first-print context: total dwelling units approved fell 3.4% to 16,710; private sector houses fell 1.0% to 10,088; private sector dwellings excluding houses fell 3.6% to 6,403; trend total dwelling units were 17,363 with 0.0% monthly change."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"ABS March 2026 Building Approvals release","result":"Fetched March 2026 first-print context: total dwellings approved fell 10.5% to 17,300; private sector dwellings excluding houses fell 26.0% to 6,632; private sector houses rose 0.9% to 10,194; trend total dwellings were 17,657 with 0.5% monthly change."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"ABS February and January 2026 Building Approvals releases","result":"Fetched February 2026 total dwellings approved rose 29.7% to 19,022 and trend rose 1.2% to 17,566; fetched January 2026 total dwellings approved fell 7.2% to 14,564 and trend was 16,386 with -0.1% monthly change."},{"kind":"text","text":"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: recent first prints show very high realized monthly dispersion, with January -7.2, February +29.7, March -10.5, and April -3.4. The central tendency is closer to low positive or flat than to the extreme February rebound."},{"kind":"text","text":"Level and momentum split: the April level of 16,710 was about 3.8% below the April trend level of 17,363, so I allow a modest bounce. Momentum is negative over March-April, so the bounce should be smaller than the February spike."},{"kind":"text","text":"Mechanism split: detached houses were comparatively stable while multi-unit approvals drove the big 2026 swings. Policy pressure to lift housing supply is an upside force, but financing, construction costs, and project feasibility keep the near-term print noisy rather than persistently strong."},{"kind":"math","text":"Point: start from a flat-to-slightly-positive base rate near +0.5%, add about +2.0 percentage points for mean reversion from April being 3.8% below trend, subtract about -0.5 percentage points for weak March-April momentum, giving +2.0%. Interval: recent monthly changes span roughly -10.5% to +29.7%; for an 80% first-print interval, use about -12/+14 percentage points around the point, skewed upward for apartment-project lumpiness, giving -10.0% to +16.0%."},{"kind":"text","text":"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would be another large apartment-project drop or delayed approvals pushing the print below -10.0%; upside outside the interval would be a renewed multi-unit approval surge like February, above +16.0%; the central case is a partial rebound with no repeat of February's one-off jump."},{"kind":"forecast","point":2.0,"ciLow":-10.0,"ciHigh":16.0}]}
